    },
    {
      // One file where no-known-value-widening is off ON PURPOSE — its widened
      // type is the honest contract, not discarded evidence:
      // - packages/contracts/src/json.ts: safeJsonParse erases to `unknown` by design;
      //   its catch arm returns a literal, which the rule rejects against any
      //   unknown-returning signature.
      // The bar for removing a file from this list is a change that keeps the
      // contract AND satisfies the rule — not a reworded cast.
      // oxlint-disarm: anti-slop/no-known-value-widening — the file's widened type is the honest contract (an erased `unknown`), not discarded evidence.
      "files": ["packages/contracts/src/json.ts"],
      "rules": {
        "anti-slop/no-known-value-widening": "off"
      }
    },
    {
      // no-unsafe-dictionary-type is at `error` for product source; test and
      // eval scopes are exempt ON PURPOSE. The rule asks an open dictionary
      // to name its value contract, which is load-bearing when the bag crosses
      // a product seam. A fixture's capture buffer or an eval's mock row has
      // no unseen contract to name — the bag IS the fixture — so the rule
      // there only extracts renames. Product source was driven to zero at warn
      // before this promotion; see docs/research/anti-slop-paydown-2026-09-12.md
      // and docs/research/narrow-record-types-2026-08-09.md. The ops-scripts
      // scopes live in the NEXT entry: a test/eval entry may not carry a
      // non-test glob, because the blanket-disarm check skips a scope covering
      // tests/evals wholesale and the non-test half would go unjudged.
      "files": [
        "**/test/**",
        "**/*.test.ts",
        "**/*.test.tsx",
        "**/*.test.mts",
        "**/*.test.cts",
        "**/evals/**",
        "**/*.eval.ts"
      ],
      "rules": {
        "anti-slop/no-unsafe-dictionary-type": "off"
      }
    },
    {
      // no-unsafe-dictionary-type stays off ON PURPOSE in the two ops-scripts
      // trees below: a one-off probe's parsed-JSON bag has no unseen contract
      // to name, and neither tree is typed product code the rule exists to
      // police. Repo-level scripts/ gates are NOT covered — each honest
      // boundary walk there carries its own per-site disable with the reason,
      // so deleting one surfaces as an unused directive rather than silence.
      // oxlint-disarm: anti-slop/no-unsafe-dictionary-type — a one-off probe's parsed-JSON bag has no unseen contract to name; ops tooling, not product seams.
      "files": ["apps/server/src/scripts/**", "packages/*/src/scripts/**"],
      "rules": {
        "anti-slop/no-unsafe-dictionary-type": "off"
      }
    },
    {
      // no-unknown-returns is at `error` for product source; test and eval
      // scopes are exempt ON PURPOSE. A fixture's stubbed row has no named
      // domain type to return — the `unknown` IS the fixture — so the rule
      // there only extracts renames. See
      // docs/research/anti-slop-paydown-2026-09-12.md. The ops-scripts scopes
      // live in the NEXT entry: a test/eval entry may not carry a non-test
      // glob, because the blanket-disarm check skips a scope covering
      // tests/evals wholesale and the non-test half would go unjudged.
      "files": [
        "**/test/**",
        "**/*.test.ts",
        "**/*.test.tsx",
        "**/*.test.mts",
        "**/*.test.cts",
        "**/evals/**",
        "**/*.eval.ts"
      ],
      "rules": {
        "anti-slop/no-unknown-returns": "off"
      }
    },
    {
      // no-unknown-returns stays off ON PURPOSE in the two ops-scripts trees
      // below: a one-off probe's parsed-JSON bag has no named domain type to
      // return, and neither tree is typed product code the rule exists to
      // police. Repo-level scripts/ gates are NOT covered — each honest
      // boundary walk there carries its own per-site disable with the reason,
      // so deleting one surfaces as an unused directive rather than silence.
      // oxlint-disarm: anti-slop/no-unknown-returns — a one-off probe's parsed-JSON bag has no named domain type to return; ops tooling, not product seams.
      "files": ["apps/server/src/scripts/**", "packages/*/src/scripts/**"],
      "rules": {
        "anti-slop/no-unknown-returns": "off"
      }
    },
    {
      // no-runtime-typeof is at `error` for product source; test and eval
      // scopes are exempt ON PURPOSE. A fixture's `typeof` probe of a
      // hand-built row or an eval's check of a stubbed response is not the
      // unparsed-value narrowing the rule exists to reject — the probe IS the
      // fixture. Product source was driven to zero at warn before this
      // promotion; see docs/research/anti-slop-paydown-2026-09-12.md. The
      // ops-scripts scopes live in the NEXT entry: a test/eval entry may not
